  <header>
    <span class="template-kicker">Field notes</span>
    <h2>What still water teaches about pace</h2>
    <p class="article-meta">Mira Halden · 8 min read · March 2026</p>
  </header>
  <div class="article-body">
    <p>
      A pond holds its shape by doing very little. Water arrives from one side,
      leaves from the other, and in between it settles enough that you can read
      the bottom.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading is the same. A page that settles gives the eye a floor to stand
      on, and the reader stops noticing the letters at all. That is the whole
      trick of body type: it should disappear while it is working.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      A heading can be loud. The paragraph under it has to be patient.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Set a long passage in a display face and the surface never stops moving.
      Set it in a face built for text and the ripples stay where you put them,
      at the title, at the pull quote, at the one word you meant to stress.
    </p>
    <p>
      Judge the pair here, at paragraph length, before you judge it on a
      specimen sheet.
    </p>
  </div>
</article>
